# x402 setup — {{projectName}}

{{description}}

This scaffold contains both halves of an x402 payment: a **seller** that answers HTTP
402 with its price, and a **buyer** that pays it. Both live in `apps/api`.

It is pointed at Celo Sepolia by default, so you can run the whole loop for free.

## What x402 actually does

An agent calls your endpoint. You answer `402 Payment Required` with a machine-readable
description of what you accept — asset, amount, recipient, network. The agent signs an
EIP-3009 authorization for exactly that and calls again. A **facilitator** verifies the
signature, submits the transfer on chain, and pays the gas. Then your handler runs.

The consequence people do not expect: **the buyer needs no CELO.** Gas is sponsored by
the facilitator, so an agent holding nothing but a stablecoin can transact. That is the
reason this is worth scaffolding rather than pasting.

Celo hosts a facilitator for each network, so you do not run one:

| Network | Facilitator | Chain |
|---|---|---|
| Celo Sepolia (default) | `https://api.x402.sepolia.celo.org` | `eip155:11142220` |
| Celo mainnet | `https://api.x402.celo.org` | `eip155:42220` |

## Get a facilitator API key

Do this first — it takes about a minute and costs nothing.

1. Open [https://x402.celo.org](https://x402.celo.org) and connect an EVM wallet.
2. Click **Create API key** and sign the message. No gas, no transaction.
3. Copy the key. It looks like `x402_…` and is **shown only once**.

New accounts start with **free credits on both mainnet and testnet**, so you can run
this whole template without buying anything. Each settlement spends one credit; you
buy more by depositing USDC on the same dashboard. If you lose the key, **Lost your
key? Regenerate it** issues a new one and keeps your credits.

The key is a **server-side secret**. It identifies you to the facilitator, is never
part of the payment, and is never sent to the buyer — **the buyer needs no key at
all.** Keep it in `.env`, which is already gitignored.

## Run it

```bash
cd apps/api
cp .env.template .env
```

Fill in three things in `.env`:

- `X402_API_KEY` — the key from the step above.
- `X402_PAY_TO` — an address you control. Until it is set, `/paid` answers for free.
- `BUYER_PRIVATE_KEY` — a **throwaway** key holding a little testnet USDC.

Then, in two terminals:

```bash
pnpm api:dev      # the seller, on http://localhost:3001
pnpm api:buy      # the buyer pays it
```

The buyer prints the settlement transaction hash and a block explorer link. If you see
one, the whole path worked: signature, verification, on-chain transfer, sponsored gas.

## The three things that go wrong

**The price is in raw units, not decimals.** USDC has 6, so `10000` is 0.01 USDC — not
10,000 USDC. This is off by a factor of a million in the direction that hurts.

**The token has to implement EIP-3009.** The `exact` scheme signs a
`transferWithAuthorization`. A token without it cannot settle through this path no
matter what you put in `asset`. USDC has it, which is why the template uses USDC.

**`extra` is the EIP-712 domain, and it cannot be read from the contract.** The `name`
and `version` in `apps/api/src/index.ts` are what the token signs with. Get them wrong
and the token rejects the signature at settlement, after everything else looked fine.

**A missing API key does not show up until settlement.** `/supported` and `/verify`
both answer without one, so the server boots and returns a correct 402. Only `/settle`
rejects, with `{"error":"unauthorized","message":"Missing X-API-Key"}`, by which point
the buyer has already signed. The seller warns about this at startup.

**Running out of credits fails at the same place.** Settlement returns `402` rather
than `401`, which reads like the payment was refused when the account balance is the
real cause. Check the dashboard before assuming the payment path broke.

## Going to mainnet

Set `X402_NETWORK=mainnet` in `.env` and fund the buyer with real USDC. Nothing else
changes — the addresses and the facilitator URL switch with it, in
`apps/api/src/x402.ts`.

Test on Sepolia first. Mainnet settlements move real money and are not reversible.
